import SwiftUI
import KwipMLCore

enum SelfPlayLessonContent {
    static let steps:[LessonContentStep]=[
        LessonContent.step("intro","What is on this canvas","This is a bounded take-away game: two players remove one, two or three objects from a pile of 21. Taking the last object wins.\n\n- [Authored game](kwip-node://data/rules) defines the rules, legal actions and one observation: the fraction of objects remaining.\n- [Shared features](kwip-node://model/shared) learns eight intermediate values.\n- [Action logits](kwip-node://model/policy) gives one score for each of the three fixed actions.\n- [Acting-player value](kwip-node://model/value) estimates the current player’s result from −1 to 1.\n- [Learn Self-play](kwip-node://control/train) searches games, collects terminal outcomes, updates this shared model and measures it against its initial weights.\n\nThe model does not write rules or change which moves are legal. This editable practice is unscored. A small run demonstrates the workflow, not game strength."),
        LessonContent.step("heads","Follow the paired outputs","Follow [Game observations](kwip-node://model/observation) through the shared trunk to the two branches. Select [Game policy and value](kwip-node://model/output). Its task is Game policy/value. Policy expects raw action scores; Value expects one bounded scalar. Tanh explicitly bounds that branch.\n\nThe value is for the player whose turn it is. If the rules grant an extra turn, the search keeps that player’s perspective rather than blindly flipping the sign.",kind:.canvasAction),
        LessonContent.step("configure","Review the learning loop","Open [Learn Self-play](kwip-node://control/train) → Configure Self-play. Review Self-play, Search, Learning and Replay & checkpoints. Validate bindings probes the actual rules/model without learning; Apply saves settings.\n\nEach iteration freezes collection weights, plays bounded search games, learns masked policy/value targets and commits a resume boundary. Truncated or failed games stay visible but supply no invented draw targets.",kind:.canvasAction),
        LessonContent.step("inspect","Run and inspect actual measurements","Select [Learn Self-play](kwip-node://control/train), then Run Flow. Metrics shows completed updates within an iteration. Switch Joint/Policy/Value loss and Updates/Iteration/Time; Pause chart freezes only the display.\n\n[Inspect iterations](kwip-node://data/iterations) and [Inspect evaluation](kwip-node://data/evaluation) use the standard table viewer. Evaluation uses independent seeds, balanced seats, the selected frozen opponents and no root noise. Compare policy-only with search-assisted results. Count truncated/failed attempts separately from terminal scores.",kind:.canvasAction),
        LessonContent.step("campaign","Continue learning across jobs","Open [Learn Self-play](kwip-node://control/train) → Configure Self-play → Campaign. A campaign keeps learning across small, bounded jobs while \(ProductBrand.name) is open and this Mac is awake. Set a finite total iteration, time and disk budget.\n\n- Pause at boundary lets the current job commit. Stop now keeps the previous committed boundary.\n- Resume continues unchanged settings. Extend uses a larger applied budget and records a new schedule phase.\n- Fork starts from the selected weights with fresh optimizer and replay; it is not exact continuation.\n- Required replay is shared once. History keeps each job’s complete tables and source; it does not substitute the current canvas. Pin a boundary before relying on its learning state long term.",kind:.canvasAction),
        LessonContent.step("compare","Compare strength and compute honestly","In Evaluation, select the initial model, random legal actions, uniform-prior search or a retained checkpoint. Optional held-out openings are sequences of legal actions. They are tested by the rules and excluded from learning replay. Compare both seats, complete pairs and failed/truncated coverage; a lower loss or a win against random is not proof of strong play.\n\nIn Compute, CPU still handles rules and search decisions. Apple GPU may handle batched model evaluations and learning updates after Validate checks the actual graph and optimizer. GPU recovery is numerically qualified, not a promise of identical CPU/GPU bits. Automatic keeps CPU unless the complete workload has a verified speed advantage. More active games or a bigger batch is not always faster.",kind:.canvasAction),
        LessonContent.step("reuse","Reuse weights or resume learning","Run [Test learned policy](kwip-node://control/session) after training to evaluate the frozen learned policy against random legal moves. It consumes the policy head and never changes weights.\n\nOn [Save paired model](kwip-node://control/save), choose a parent folder and give the new model folder an unused name, then choose Save model to export both heads, architecture and provenance as a nonresumable portable inference package. Required replay/optimizer/device RNG remain with the recorded training run. Resume committed boundary in Train starts a new owned operation and verifies exact pins; changing rules or encoding requires a fresh run. Save the practice and return to the Library when finished.",kind:.canvasAction)
    ]
}
struct SelfPlayLearningView:View {
    @ObservedObject var store:WorkspaceStore
    var body:some View {Button("Open Self-play practice"){_=store.loadComponentWorkflowExample(SelfPlayPractice.lessonID,device:"cpu")}.buttonStyle(FlatButtonStyle()).disabled(store.isBusy)}
}
